== 4. Restore round-trip (goal task 4) ==================================

$ scripts/litestream-restore.sh          # operator host
[litestream-restore] checking /etc/dg/litestream.env and LITESTREAM_S3_BUCKET on micro2 (as dg)
[litestream-restore] listing registered live databases on micro2
[litestream-restore] restore output directory: /tmp/lit-restore (local; scratch on micro2: /var/tmp/lit-restore-1792085191)
[litestream-restore] [verify_probe] litestream restore (as dg, /etc/litestream.yml) -> /var/tmp/lit-restore-1792085191/verify_probe.sqlite on micro2
[litestream-restore] [verify_probe] pulling the restored file back for inspection
[litestream-restore] [verify_probe] OK: integrity_check=ok, all 1 table row counts match
[litestream-restore] removing the scratch dir on micro2
[litestream-restore] summary: 1 ok, 0 failed (of 1 registered db(s): verify_probe)
[litestream-restore] all live db(s) restored; local copies kept in /tmp/lit-restore (mode 0700)
rc=0

The comparison covers the user tables (the gateway-served data): SQLite
sqlite_* plus the internal _dg_* / _litestream_* families are excluded on
both sides because the dg CLI answers sql_forbidden (403) for internal
tables by design; PRAGMA integrity_check covers the whole restored file.

$ cargo test --test litestream_restore
running 4 tests
test integrity_check_passes_on_a_fresh_db ... ok
test integrity_check_fails_on_a_corrupted_db ... ok
test row_counts_diverge_when_a_row_is_missing ... ok
test row_counts_match_when_the_copies_are_identical ... ok
test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out

== 5. Monitoring (objective clause "monitor it") ========================

scripts/host-audit.sh now probes dg-litestream.service (active, User=dg,
Restart=on-failure), so a dead or mis-configured replicator is a live
verdict=fail in the read-only fleet audit instead of silent staleness.

$ DG_AUDIT_TARGET=micro2 scripts/host-audit.sh
verdict: pass | host_side: pass
  dg_binary_sha256: pass
  dg_service: pass
  dg_backup_timer: pass
  dg_litestream_service: pass
  micro2_backup_script_sha256: pass
  stale_staging_dirs: pass
  latest_set: pass
  backup_verify: pass
  owner_key_mode: pass
failures: []

== 6. Credential confinement ============================================

$ scripts/check-litestream-secret.sh        # ~5 min: reads every file under the scanned roots
[check-litestream-secret] only copy verified: /etc/dg/litestream.env (roots: /etc /home /root /opt /srv /var/lib /var/backups /var/tmp) (target: micro2)

The secret is read only inside the host process, into a mode-0600 file in
/run (tmpfs, outside the scanned roots), matched with `grep -ralFf
PATTERN_FILE`, and the pattern file removed; no secret passes through
argv, so sudo's COMMAND= journal never sees it.

== Script correctness notes (what this goal fixed in the staged artefacts) ==========

* litestream 0.5.17 `restore` rejects -config together with a replica URL
  ("cannot specify a replica URL and the -config flag"), and URL mode
  reads litestream's own env names rather than this deployment's
  LITESTREAM_S3_* names. The script now restores by DB_PATH with -config,
  so the ${LITESTREAM_S3_*} references expand from the sidecar env exactly
  as the running service does.
* The restore script's live-db discovery was a `*.sqlite` glob, which
  picks up metadata.sqlite -- the one database with no replica by design.
  It now uses the registry (`dg list`), matching the installer.
* dg CLI calls ship the SQL base64-encoded over ssh; plain embedding let
  the two shell layers eat the SQL's inner double quotes (surfaced as an
  opaque dg api_error).
* The restored file is pulled back with `sudo cat` over ssh rather than
  scp: the remote copy stays 0600 in a 0700 directory instead of being
  loosened so the login user can read it.
* check-litestream-secret.sh previously read every file fully in python
  and could not finish inside any operator timeout (>5 min, ~6GB/~380k
  files); it now greps the same bytes with the same leak-free pattern
  handling.
